# speedtest-go: an Ookla (speedtest.net) client in Go. Fully static without
# asking, because CGO is off.
#
# Pinned to a release tag rather than built with `go install pkg@latest`, which
# is what the recipe this replaces did: @latest has no checksum and produces a
# different binary every time upstream pushes. The tag tarball carries a
# SHA-256, and the module's own go.sum pins every dependency below it.
PKG=speedtest
VERSION=1.8.3
REVISION=1
SOURCE=https://github.com/showwin/speedtest-go/archive/refs/tags/v1.8.3.tar.gz
SHA256=48d01137468da9d419a3940a652803dafd8a6820abcd985b85c9d0c86b417ba3
TOOLCHAIN=go
# No armv7-neon: Go's GOARM=7 has no NEON variant, so that build would be
# byte-identical to armv7 -- verified, same SHA-256 -- and it could not satisfy
# the NEON gate either, because Go emits no ARM build attributes at all. One
# armv7 build serves both kinds of box; detect.sh --all degrades to it.
TARGETS='x86_64 i686 armv5 armv7 aarch64 mips mipsel'

SMOKE='--version'
SMOKE_EXPECT='speedtest-go'

# UNVERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE: Go's documented floor is a newer kernel than the
# 2.6.32 a VU+ Solo2 runs. Under qemu-mipsel with `-r 2.6.32` the binary starts
# and prints its version, but that only shows Go does not check uname and
# refuse -- qemu still services the syscalls from the host kernel, so it proves
# nothing about which syscalls a real 2.6.32 has. The only real test is the box.
# If it fails there, a small LibreSpeed client in C or Zig is the way out: that
# protocol is far simpler than Ookla's, and a native binary has no kernel floor.
